// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-11-04 (Wednesday): Today's date as pattern
// ============================================================================
// Weekly rotation slot: the calendar date itself drives the pattern. Today
// (IST) is 2026-11-04 -- day 4, month 11 (November), year 2026.
//
// THE DERIVATION (this is the whole point of the day):
//   Recent Wednesdays each found a different relationship between day and
//   month: a digit sequence (08-26), clock rates (09-02), a hocket when
//   day==month (09-09), coprime grid-lengths (09-16), per-token Euclidean
//   flutters (09-23), a GCD-reduced polyrhythm (09-30), day/month fed into
//   euclid(7,10) (10-07), binary bits compared (10-14), the Euclidean
//   algorithm's own quotient/remainder steps (10-21), and a perfect-number
//   vs. deficient-number divisor-sum contrast (10-28). Today's day (4) and
//   month (11) are ALSO coprime, like several of those -- but the pair has
//   its own, more specific property none of those used: 4 = 2x2 = 2^2 is a
//   PERFECT SQUARE (the smallest one that admits a genuine 2-row-by-2-column
//   grid), while 11 is PRIME -- it has no factorisation at all besides
//   1x11, so nothing about it can be arranged into equal groups. Squareness
//   (groupable into a grid two ways) versus primality (ungroupable, full
//   stop) is a structural contrast none of the prior GCD/ratio/binary/
//   divisor-sum devices made -- those compared SUMS or BITS; this compares
//   whether a number FACTORS into equal parts at all.
//
//   A genuine coincidence specific to today, used as the anchor: day (4)
//   mod 7 = 4, and month (11) mod 7 = 4 as well -- the SAME residue. Every
//   prior Wednesday using this project's day/month-mod-7 mapping landed on
//   two different anchor tones (tonic vs degree 3, etc.); today day and
//   month share one. So DAY and MONTH both anchor on scale degree 4 here --
//   convergence instead of contrast on the anchor tone, with the squareness/
//   primality split carrying the actual structural contrast instead.
//
//   DAY (the perfect square, 4 = 2x2): built as a literal 2-row-by-2-column
//   grid of four 2-note cells, each cell opening on the shared anchor
//   (degree 4) before diverging up or down:
//     A = "4 7"   B = "10 7"      (row 0)
//     C = "2 4"   D = "-1 4"      (row 1)
//   CALL reads the grid row-major (A B, rest, C D) -- the "normal" reading
//   order. VARIATION reads the SAME grid column-major (A C, rest, B D) --
//   a literal matrix transpose, swapping rows for columns, which is only a
//   non-trivial, legible operation because the grid is square (a 2x2
//   transpose has real content; a 1x4 "grid" wouldn't). RESPONSE is CALL's
//   exact full-token retrograde (the whole 9-step bar reversed) -- a third,
//   independent device (true retrograde) alongside the transpose, giving
//   this day two distinct motif-and-variation operations instead of one.
//
//   MONTH (the prime, 11): because 11 has no non-trivial factors, its
//   phrase is written as ONE continuous, never-internally-grouped run of
//   exactly 11 tokens -- no brackets, no equal subdivision, in direct
//   contrast to DAY's doubly-groupable grid. Deliberately NOT length-matched
//   to DAY's 9-step bar (several prior Wednesdays synced both bars to the
//   same step count; doing that here would force 11's irreducible run into
//   an arbitrary shared length and erase the whole point). M1 is the base
//   11-token run, opening on the shared anchor; M2 is M1's full retrograde
//   (reversal preserves indivisibility -- a reversed prime-length run is
//   still irreducible); M3 is M1 cyclically rotated by 1 step -- a fact
//   that only reads cleanly because 11 is prime: a 1-step rotation of a
//   prime-length sequence can never align with a shorter repeating unit,
//   unlike rotating a composite-length run (4, 8, 28...) which can expose
//   smaller sub-periods. The three MONTH phrases cycle in their own
//   <...> alternator, independently of DAY's.
//
//   CYCLES = 25 = day-digit-sum (4) + month (11) + year-digit-sum
//   (2+0+2+6=10). setcpm(75) x 25 cycles / 60 = 20s.
//
// Scale: D minor -- fresh for this slot (recent Wednesdays used E, G, C, B,
// and F minor); the squareness/primality contrast is the idea, not the mode.
// D minor's own fifth (A) is scale degree 4 -- so the shared day/month
// anchor tone this week is literally the drone's own fifth, not a separate
// coincidence layered on top of the tanpura convention.
//
// NO DRUM KIT, deliberately, not by default: the grid-transpose/retrograde
// structure on DAY and the irreducible single-strand run on MONTH ARE this
// piece's audible rhythm, read straight off today's own day/month factor
// structure -- a metric kit underneath would just restate a pulse that's
// already there, the reflexive-percussion failure MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md flags.
//
// Instrumentation, a fresh pairing from the last two Wednesdays (pulse+
// triangle on 10-28, square+sawtooth on 10-21), chosen for what each
// number IS rather than habit: DAY (sawtooth) gets a synth basic with a
// full, unbroken harmonic series -- fitting the grid that uses all four of
// its cells, nothing missing, the "complete" voice. MONTH (pulse) gets a
// narrower, hollower synth basic -- its thinner harmonic content fits the
// single unbroken strand that cannot be split into parts. DRONE stays
// `sine` -- tanpura convention, tonic and fifth, no automation (§2.5, §3
// of MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md). Only DAY carries the acid taste layer (light
// distortion + filter sweep); the sweep period (2 cycles) is sqrt(4) --
// day's own square root, tying the one automated parameter back to the
// squareness device itself rather than a number borrowed from month or a
// generic GCD.
//
// This is a DAILY quick clip, not a flagship set -- one continuous pattern,
// no alap/jor/jhala arc.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(75)
let CYCLES = 25 // day-digit-sum (4) + month (11) + year-digit-sum (10)

stack(
  // DAY -- the perfect square (4 = 2x2), anchored on the shared residue
  // (4 mod 7 = 4). Four 2-note cells (A,B top row; C,D bottom row) read
  // three ways: CALL is the grid row-major; RESPONSE is CALL's exact full
  // retrograde (reversed token-for-token); VARIATION is the SAME grid
  // read column-major -- a literal matrix transpose, legible only because
  // the grid is square.
  n("<[4 7 10 7 ~ 2 4 -1 4] [4 -1 4 2 ~ 7 10 7 4] [4 7 2 4 ~ 10 7 -1 4]>")
    .scale("D:minor")
    .sound("sawtooth")
    .attack(.006).decay(.11).sustain(0).release(.05)
    .distort(.15) // acid taste layer, kept light so the grid/retrograde stays legible
    .lpf(sine.range(500, 2400).slow(2)) // sweep period = sqrt(4) = day's own square root
    .gain(.32)
    .pan(.68)
    .room(1.1),

  // MONTH -- the prime (11), irreducible: one continuous 11-token run,
  // never internally grouped, deliberately a different length from DAY's
  // 9-step bar (forcing it to match would erase the point). M1 is the base
  // run; M2 is M1's full retrograde (still irreducible, reversed); M3 is
  // M1 rotated by one step -- a rotation that can't expose a shorter
  // sub-period precisely because 11 is prime.
  n("<[4 ~ 7 2 10 4 -1 7 4 2 4] [4 2 4 7 -1 4 10 2 7 ~ 4] [4 4 ~ 7 2 10 4 -1 7 4 2]>")
    .scale("D:minor")
    .sound("pulse")
    .attack(.04).decay(.3).sustain(.15).release(.9)
    .gain(.27)
    .pan(.32)
    .room(2.6),

  // DRONE -- tanpura convention, tonic/fifth, held for the full piece with
  // no automation (the one moving filter this week lives on DAY instead).
  // D minor's fifth (A) is scale degree 4 -- the same tone DAY and MONTH
  // both anchor on this week.
  note("[d2,a2]/25")
    .sound("sine")
    .attack(1.4).release(2)
    .gain(.22)
    .pan(.5)
    .room(3.2)
)
